---
/**
 * foodcraft / FooterBM — "Tahanan Cafe" · THE WHITE CLOSING PAGE.
 *
 * ROLE: the last place a visitor looks for the name, the phone, the email and
 * the address before they give up. So every cell here is real owner data or the
 * cell is absent.
 *
 * WHAT THE DESIGN DRAWS (reproduced value for value from the FOOTER element of
 * "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html", nothing re-derived):
 *   a PLAIN WHITE ground — no band, no tint — 56px of air, then a
 *   minmax(0,1.6fr) / minmax(0,1fr) / minmax(0,1fr) grid at a 38px gap:
 *   the wordmark lock with its 9px walnut town tag, a 36ch blurb and one 13px
 *   walnut action; a page-links column; a contact column. The grid is CLOSED by
 *   a hairline 36px under it — this design draws its structure with borders,
 *   not gaps — and under that, 20px down, a two-cell colophon of 10px caps at
 *   .22em, space-between.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string in this file, and nothing else. Each is
 * declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts, so each is a hook a
 * sidebar input actually owns (a data-field on any other path is a dead hook):
 *   footer.brand                     — the 24px Libre Caslon wordmark, and the
 *                                      colophon's identity half (HeaderBM's
 *                                      wordmark too: one field, three places)
 *   footer.locality                  — the 9px walnut town tag on its baseline
 *   footer.blurb                     — the standing line under the lock
 *   footer.explore.title             — the LINKS column heading
 *   footer.visit.title               — the CONTACT column heading
 *   footer.explore.links.${i}.text   — a links-column label, when the owner
 *   footer.explore.links.${i}.href     filled the footer's own links list
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label          — a links-column label, when the column is
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href             falling back to the nav array (see D2)
 *   navCtaText                       — the walnut action's label (see D3)
 *   navCtaHref                       — its target (data-href-field)
 *   location.phone                   — the tel: row (data-field only, D6)
 *   contact.email                    — the mailto: row (D7)
 *   location.address                 — the address line, and the colophon's
 *                                      other half
 *   footer.notes.${i}                — the colophon's RIGHT cell (see D1)
 *
 * DERIVED here, never authored here:
 *   · the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when footer.brand is
 *     blank — the same name out of the same submission, not an invention.
 *   · the blurb falls back to footer.brand_blurb, the schema's own alias.
 *   · phone, email and address fall back to layout.contact.* — which IS
 *     content.contact, so an owner who filled the footer group's own
 *     "Email"/"Address" boxes still gets those rows, and no two sections on
 *     this page can print different numbers or different addresses.
 *   · the tel: href is built from the phone's own digits; the mailto: href is
 *     built from the email. Neither prefix is ever stored.
 *   · every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBM really
 *     renders. The helper, PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS are byte-identical to
 *     HeaderBM's, so the rail and the footer can never send a visitor to two
 *     different places.
 *   · `i` on every list row is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index (R6), so dropping
 *     an unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *   · the colophon's address is FLATTENED to one line (newlines to commas) to
 *     fit a single row of tracked caps — which is why that node, and only that
 *     node, carries data-derived: editorBridge's live ed:update would otherwise
 *     paint the raw multi-line scalar into a caps row. The COLUMN's address is
 *     NOT derived: it is the stored string verbatim, broken by
 *     `white-space: pre-line`, so an inline edit there stays truthful.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "Tahanan", "San Juan", "This page", "Get in
 *     touch", "The menu", "Our story", "Gallery", "FAQ", "Contact us", the
 *     blurb, "9 Pinaglabanan St, San Juan" and "Prices in Philippine Pesos" are
 *     the DESIGNER filling in their own template. Not one of those words is in
 *     this file. A café that supplied no nav links gets no links column, not
 *     the designer's four.
 *   · a column heading with no column under it, a lock with no name, a blurb
 *     with no text, a link with no label or no reachable target, an action with
 *     no label, a colophon with nothing in it, and the whole footer when all of
 *     the above are empty. EVERY guard sits outside its tag, so a blank value
 *     removes the ELEMENT and not just its text — no heading over nothing, no
 *     hairline under a row that is not there.
 *   · THE GRID'S CLOSING HAIRLINE when nothing follows it. The design draws
 *     that rule BETWEEN the grid and the colophon, so it rides a modifier
 *     (.tcbm-fgrid--ruled) the markup adds only when the colophon renders. A
 *     site with a phone but no name and no address would otherwise close on a
 *     cream hairline floating 38px above the bottom of the page.
 *   · social chips: the design's footer draws none.
 *   · an hours block: the design's footer has none; LocationBM owns the hours.
 *   · .reveal. The footer is the page terminus; fading in the last thing on
 *     screen buys nothing and risks a footer that never un-hides.
 *   · any star row, rating or numeral — R2's star rule has nothing to bite on
 *     here: there is not one node on this band that could print a claim the
 *     owner did not make.
 *
 * DECISIONS a reader would otherwise take for an oversight
 *
 * D1. THE COLOPHON'S RIGHT CELL IS footer.notes, NOT THE CURRENCY SENTENCE.
 *     The design draws `Prices in {{ currencyName }}` there. That is a claim
 *     about how this business quotes and no field carries it, so the sentence
 *     is gone — but the CELL is not, because footer.notes is declared in this
 *     very group, labelled "Footer notes (copyright row)", and
 *     lib/derive-content-defaults.ts seeds it to `© <year> <name>` from the
 *     owner's own submitted name (and deliberately suppresses a bare "© 2026"
 *     with no name). So the design's two-cell row survives with the owner's own
 *     small print in it. A note that merely repeats the left cell VERBATIM
 *     (same string as the wordmark, or as the flattened address) is dropped —
 *     an exact-match test only, because "© 2026 Tahanan" is different
 *     information from "Tahanan · 9 Pinaglabanan St" and both belong on the
 *     row. FLAG FOR REVIEW: FooterBL dropped this cell outright rather than
 *     binding footer.notes; if that precedent is the house rule, delete the
 *     notes block and the row falls back to one left-aligned cell.
 *
 * D2. THE LINKS COLUMN HAS THREE POSSIBLE SOURCES AND ONLY TWO HOOK SHAPES.
 *     footer.explore.links is the schema's own list for this column, so it wins
 *     when the owner filled it. Otherwise the column mirrors the rail, reading
 *     layout.navLinks — the same array HeaderBM reads, so the two can never
 *     disagree after a builder fallback — and hooks `navbar_links.${i}.*`,
 *     which is the path the sidebar owns for that array. The third case is the
 *     one to notice: lib/astro-builder.ts substitutes its OWN four links
 *     ("About / Services / Gallery / Contact") when content.navbar_links is
 *     absent entirely. Those labels are nobody's words, so in that case the
 *     rows render with NO hook rather than pointing an editor at a path that
 *     holds nothing. Astro omits an undefined attribute, so this costs no
 *     markup.
 *
 * D3. THE ACTION IS DRAWN ONCE, NOT TWICE. The design draws contactLabel in
 *     BOTH the brand column and the contact column, and that reads correctly
 *     there because the designer set contactLabel to "Contact us" and headed
 *     the column "Get in touch". On a real site both come from
 *     derive-content-defaults, which seeds navCtaText to "Get in touch" — so
 *     ONE field would print the same three words twice, six lines apart. The
 *     rule here:
 *       · the action rides the BRAND column whenever that column exists on its
 *         own merits (a wordmark, a town tag or a blurb);
 *       · it falls to the CONTACT column only when the brand column does not
 *         render at all — which is where the design also draws it;
 *       · and never in the contact column when it merely restates that
 *         column's heading, compared case- and whitespace-insensitively,
 *         because "Get In Touch" is the same stutter.
 *     The brand gate counts only the lock and the blurb, NOT ctaText, so the
 *     fallback branch stays reachable (folding ctaText into that gate is what
 *     made the equivalent branch dead code in an earlier family).
 *     The pair hooked is navCtaText / navCtaHref, from the Header group, not
 *     hero.cta1: components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle by
 *     counting hooks of a given prefix inside closest('section,footer'), so a
 *     footer carrying hero.* hooks becomes a candidate for "hide the Hero
 *     block". navCtaText has no entry in that PREFIX map at all, so it is
 *     toggle-inert. HeaderBM hooks the same pair for the same reason.
 *
 * D4. THE COLUMN HEADINGS ARE NOT .kick, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN, NOT A LAPSE.
 *     The underlined eyebrow is one of this template's two signatures, and the
 *     rule for it lives on the shared primitive precisely so no section can
 *     lose it. But the design's FOOTER headings carry no rule at all: they are
 *     10px walnut caps at .26em and nothing more. Restating them here is
 *     therefore reproducing the design, not restating the eyebrow. (Even if
 *     they wanted it, .kick could not reach them: every .kick declaration in
 *     PageBM sits inside :where() at zero specificity, while
 *     `html[data-page="BM"] :where(h1,h2,h3,h4)` scores (0,1,0) and pins
 *     family, weight, colour, tracking, line-height, text-transform and margin.
 *     A scoped class compiles with the data-astro-cid attribute and wins.)
 *     They stay <h4> because two real headings give the columns a structure a
 *     screen-reader user can navigate; the design's plain <div> does not.
 *
 * D5. NO .btn IN THIS FILE, AT ALL (R4). lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts emits
 *     `.testi .btn, .cta-band .btn, footer .btn { color: <on-ink> !important }`
 *     — so a .btn anywhere under this <footer> would take a label colour picked
 *     for an INK ground and vanish on this white one. The design's footer has
 *     no button anyway (a 13px text action and 14px rows), so the action is
 *     .linkc and the rows are plain anchors, neither of which the engine
 *     touches. None of the forbidden labels (.btn-primary, .btn-ghost,
 *     .nav-cta, .cta-band, .trust, .testi) appears either.
 *
 * D6. THE tel: ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY, no data-href-field. Its href is
 *     derived from the field's own value, so a href hook there would write
 *     "tel:+63…" straight back into the phone box. Same for the mailto: row.
 *
 * D7. THE EMAIL ROW IS AN ADDITION. The design draws none — its demo café took
 *     enquiries on Messenger — but contact.email is schema-declared in the very
 *     group this footer belongs to, and a real business that supplies one had
 *     it appear NOWHERE on the page. It takes the design's own 14px row
 *     treatment, so nothing new is invented visually.
 *
 * D8. PHONE AND EMAIL ARE UNDERLINED, THE ADDRESS IS NOT. They sit directly
 *     above a static address line of the same family, size and weight that
 *     differs only in colour, and colour alone does not distinguish a link
 *     (WCAG 1.4.1). text-decoration adds no box, so thickening it on hover
 *     cannot shift the rows. Deliberately NOT applied to the links column:
 *     those are wayfinding links in a list, unambiguous by position and
 *     heading, and the design draws them bare.
 *
 * D9. NO TOP HAIRLINE. Every other seam in this design is a rule, so its
 *     absence here looks like an omission. It is not: the design's footer is a
 *     white block sitting directly under the dark CtaBand, and the band edge IS
 *     the divider. Adding a --line rule would draw a cream hairline across the
 *     top of a page that never had one.
 *
 * D10. THE ROWS ARE BLOCKS, NOT THE DESIGN'S <p>. The design uses
 *      `<p style="margin:0 0 9px">` per row, last row margin:0, over a body
 *      with no declared line-height — about a 26px pitch. Here each row is a
 *      block anchor at 14px/1.5 with 3px of padding either side: the same ~27px
 *      pitch, the whole row is the tap target, and no trailing margin dangles
 *      above the closing hairline.
 *
 * D11. THE ACTION SITS ~11px LOWER THAN DRAWN. The blurb keeps the design's
 *      16px bottom margin verbatim, and .linkc's own min-height:44px — the
 *      wrapper's tap-target floor — adds roughly 11px of optical space under
 *      it. Trimming the margin to compensate would make this file disagree with
 *      the design in order to correct something that moves the moment .linkc
 *      does, so the design's value ships and the accessibility floor stands.
 *
 * COLOUR — wrapper tokens only, no literal hex anywhere (R1). Every colour in
 * this design's footer has a token with the SAME value:
 *   #FFFFFF --paper (the ground) · #EDE6DF --line (the closing hairline)
 *   #241B14 --ink (wordmark) · #7A4E2D --accent (town tag, headings, action)
 *   #5C5147 --ink-soft (blurb, every link row) · #6B6057 --muted (the address
 *   line and the colophon caps) · #573418 --accent-deep (the design's own
 *   stated hover: its stylesheet declares a:hover{color:#573418}, shadowed in
 *   the demo only by the inline colour on each footer link).
 * Nothing needed re-deriving and nothing was.
 *
 * CONTRAST on this white ground, from PageBM's verified table: ink 14.9 ·
 * ink-soft 7.29 · accent 7.06 · muted 5.71. Every run on this band clears AA,
 * including the 10px colophon.
 *
 * CLASS NAMES are `tcbm-f*`, this template's own prefix (HeaderBM uses `tcbm-`;
 * FooterBL's `fb-` set appears nowhere here). An audit had to rename a whole
 * set copied verbatim between templates in this family — nothing in this file
 * shares a name with another template's stylesheet.
 *
 * Visibility: PageBM gates this file on visibility.footerSection — never here.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const c = (content as any) || {};
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const f = c.footer || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD — byte-identical to HeaderBM's, so the rail and the footer can
// never send a visitor to different places. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts
// hard-codes nav links to '#about', '#services', '#why', '#work', '#visit' and
// navCtaHref to '#visit'; lib/astro-builder.ts falls back to '#about',
// '#services', '#gallery', '#contact'. Most of those ids no design renders.
// Resolve every in-page href against what PageBM ACTUALLY renders: keep it if
// the id exists, remap it when this design carries the same material under
// another name, otherwise fall back — and the links column passes '' as the
// fallback, because a dead entry in a wayfinding list is worse than a missing
// one. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http(s), anything without a leading '#')
// pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames four derived targets: the services block IS the menu
// (rooms lands there too), '#about' is the café's own story (host and why are
// the same block here), '#work' is the photo wall, and '#location' / '#contact'
// both mean the visit block — address, map and opening hours. A booking link
// has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. `||`, never `??`, and identical to HeaderBM's resolution:
// genericContentSchema gives footer.brand fallbackPaths ['business_name'] and
// ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks treats '' as empty, so a CLEARED brand
// box still shows the business name in the sidebar. With `??` an empty string
// would be a value and the page would go wordmark-less while the sidebar
// displayed a name. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real customer's own mark;
// with nothing supplied the lock is omitted instead of inventing a name.
const brand = String(f.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// One field, three consumers (the rail, this lock, the map caption). Nothing
// derives it, so on a first build it is simply absent rather than guessed out
// of the address.
const locality = String(f.locality ?? '').trim();
// footer.brand_blurb is the schema's declared alias for the same box.
const blurb = String(f.blurb || f.brand_blurb || '').trim();

// The page's one action, from the Header group (see D3). '#visit' is the
// fallback because that is where this design puts the address, the map and the
// opening hours, and it is the id the derived default already names.
const ctaText = String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
const ctaHref = onPage(c.navCtaHref, '#visit');
// Computed, never literal, and ONE conditional per attribute — an owner may
// point this at #visit or a tel:, and forcing those into a new tab is wrong.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// Column headings — bound, not hardcoded, and guarded, so an owner who clears
// the box gets a column with no heading rather than the designer's word. The
// schema's own owner-facing labels decide which feeds which: footer.explore.title
// is labelled "Links column heading" (placeholder "This page") and
// footer.visit.title "Contact column heading" (placeholder "Get in touch") —
// which are this design's two headings verbatim.
const linksTitle = String(f.explore?.title ?? '').trim();
const contactTitle = String(f.visit?.title ?? '').trim();

// ── THE LINKS COLUMN (D2). Three sources, two hook shapes, one index rule.
const exploreFromAdmin = Array.isArray(f.explore?.links) && f.explore.links.length > 0;
// content.navbar_links is what the sidebar owns; lay.navLinks is that same array
// passed through by the builder, or the builder's OWN four links when content
// carries none. Reading lay.navLinks keeps the rail and the footer identical;
// testing content tells us whether a hook would land on anything.
const navFromContent = Array.isArray(c.navbar_links) && c.navbar_links.length > 0;
type LinkRow = { label: string; href: string; i: number; labelHook?: string; hrefHook?: string };
const columnLinks: LinkRow[] = ((exploreFromAdmin
  ? f.explore.links.map((l: any) => ({ label: l?.text, href: l?.href }))
  : (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : [])) as any[])
  .map((l: any, i: number): LinkRow => ({
    label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(),
    href: onPage(l?.href, ''),
    i,
    // undefined when nothing owns the path — Astro omits an undefined
    // attribute, so those rows simply carry no hook.
    labelHook: exploreFromAdmin
      ? `footer.explore.links.${i}.text`
      : (navFromContent ? `navbar_links.${i}.label` : undefined),
    hrefHook: exploreFromAdmin
      ? `footer.explore.links.${i}.href`
      : (navFromContent ? `navbar_links.${i}.href` : undefined),
  }))
  .filter((l: LinkRow) => Boolean(l.label && l.href));

// Contact rows. layout.contact IS content.contact (astro-builder passes it
// through with a display-formatted phone), so these fallbacks also pick up the
// footer group's own "Email" / "Address" boxes, and no two sections on this
// page can print different numbers or different addresses.
const phone = String(c.location?.phone || lay.contact?.phone || '').trim();
const email = String(c.contact?.email || lay.contact?.email || '').trim();
const address = String(c.location?.address || lay.contact?.address || '').trim();
// Built, never stored (D6).
const phoneDigits = phone.replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '');
// Colophon identity line: the design prints one line, and location.address is a
// multi-line textarea. Flattened for THIS row only (hence data-derived on it).
const addressFlat = address.replace(/\s*\n+\s*/g, ', ');

const norm = (v: string): string => v.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim().toLowerCase();

// Colophon right cell (D1). Empty rows go, and so does a note that is an EXACT
// repeat of what the left cell already prints — nothing cleverer, because a ©
// line that happens to contain the name is different information from the
// name-and-address line beside it.
type NoteRow = { v: string; i: number };
const notes: NoteRow[] = (Array.isArray(f.notes) ? f.notes : [])
  .map((n: any, i: number): NoteRow => ({ v: String(n ?? '').trim(), i }))
  .filter((n: NoteRow) => Boolean(n.v) && norm(n.v) !== norm(brand) && norm(n.v) !== norm(addressFlat));

// ── GATES — BODY content only (R5). The two column headings never count: a
// heading is not a column, and one printed over nothing is the exact failure
// the rule exists for. footer.brand / footer.blurb / navbar_links / navCtaText /
// footer.notes ARE seeded by derive-content-defaults, but from the owner's own
// submitted name, city and business type — that is their data, not an invented
// headline.
const hasBrandCol = Boolean(brand || locality || blurb);
// D3: one action, and never a stutter against the heading beside it.
const brandShowsCta = Boolean(ctaText) && hasBrandCol;
const showContactCta =
  Boolean(ctaText) && !brandShowsCta && norm(ctaText) !== norm(contactTitle);
const hasContactCol = Boolean(showContactCta || phone || email || address);
const hasGrid = hasBrandCol || columnLinks.length > 0 || hasContactCol;
const hasColophon = Boolean(brand || addressFlat || notes.length > 0);
---
{(hasGrid || hasColophon) && (
<footer class="tcbm-foot">
  <div class="wrap">
    {hasGrid && (
      <div class:list={['tcbm-fgrid', hasColophon && 'tcbm-fgrid--ruled']}>
        {hasBrandCol && (
          <div class="tcbm-fbrand">
            {(brand || locality) && (
              <div class="tcbm-flock">
                {brand && <span class="tcbm-fmark" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
                {locality && <span class="tcbm-ftown" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
              </div>
            )}
            {blurb && <p class="tcbm-fblurb" data-field="footer.blurb">{blurb}</p>}
            {/* Both hooks sit on the ANCHOR: the editor's link popover reads
                data-field from whatever carries data-href-field, so a label on
                an inner span would leave it editing an empty path. The arrow is
                generated content — editorBridge writes an inline edit back
                through innerHTML, so a glyph inside the element could end up
                stored in navCtaText; a pseudo-element cannot. */}
            {brandShowsCta && (
              <a
                class="linkc tcbm-fcta"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="navCtaText"
                data-href-field="navCtaHref"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}

        {columnLinks.length > 0 && (
          <div class="tcbm-fcol">
            {linksTitle && <h4 class="tcbm-fh" data-field="footer.explore.title">{linksTitle}</h4>}
            {columnLinks.map((l) => (
              <a
                class="tcbm-frow"
                href={l.href}
                data-field={l.labelHook}
                data-href-field={l.hrefHook}
              >{l.label}</a>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}

        {hasContactCol && (
          <div class="tcbm-fcol">
            {contactTitle && <h4 class="tcbm-fh" data-field="footer.visit.title">{contactTitle}</h4>}
            {showContactCta && (
              <a
                class="tcbm-frow"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="navCtaText"
                data-href-field="navCtaHref"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
            {(phone || email || address) && (
              <address class="tcbm-faddr">
                {phone && (
                  <a class="tcbm-frow tcbm-fchan" href={`tel:${phoneDigits}`} data-field="location.phone">{phone}</a>
                )}
                {/* mailto: is BUILT, never stored — the visible text stays the
                    plain address so an inline edit can never write "mailto:…"
                    back into the email box. */}
                {email && (
                  <a class="tcbm-frow tcbm-fchan" href={`mailto:${email}`} data-field="contact.email">{email}</a>
                )}
                {/* The stored string verbatim — `white-space: pre-line` draws
                    the owner's own line breaks, so the DOM text and the field
                    value stay identical and a live edit cannot lie. */}
                {address && <span class="tcbm-fline" data-field="location.address">{address}</span>}
              </address>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}

    {hasColophon && (
      <div class="mono tcbm-fcolo">
        {(brand || addressFlat) && (
          <span class="tcbm-fid">
            {brand && <span data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
            {brand && addressFlat && <span class="tcbm-fsep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>}
            {addressFlat && <span data-field="location.address" data-derived>{addressFlat}</span>}
          </span>
        )}
        {notes.length > 0 && (
          <span class="tcbm-fnotes">
            {notes.map((n, d) => (
              <>
                {d > 0 && <span class="tcbm-fsep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>}
                <span data-field={`footer.notes.${n.i}`}>{n.v}</span>
              </>
            ))}
          </span>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</footer>
)}
<style>
  /* The design's closing page: a plain --paper ground, 56px of air above the
     grid, 38px below the colophon, and NO top rule (see D9 in the frontmatter —
     the dark CtaBand above is the divider). The wrapper's `section` padding
     rule targets <section> only, so all of this padding is this file's. */
  .tcbm-foot {
    background-color: var(--paper);
